+def _run_probe(action, params, probes):
+    """``run_probe``: run one declared read-only command, report it verbatim.
+
+    The ``command`` parameter *names* a declaration; it is never itself
+    executed, split, or composed. The argv that runs is the argv the
+    operator declared for that name, unchanged, through
+    :func:`driver_core.osal.run` -- an argv list, no shell -- so there is
+    no composition for a hostile name to exploit: an undeclared name is
+    simply refused, and a declared one can only ever run as declared.
+
+    A non-zero exit is an answer, not a refusal: a check script that
+    exits 3 has told the verifier something, and dressing that up as an
+    executor failure would hide it. A probe that could not be *asked*
+    (missing binary, timeout) is refused instead, because its result
+    carries no real exit code to report.
